#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120663} :activity/id #uuid "f2dd4c26-139a-5928-934f-ad8833466f2b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049088 :topic/id #uuid "d97a4040-06bd-5a66-adf2-e1405a5fc0e6" :topic/math-academy-id 1782} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120663 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121031 :tutorial/content "There is an algorithm that uses $LU$ factorization to solve systems of equations of the form \n\n$$\nA\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b} .\n$$\n\nFor example, let's solve the following equation:\n\n![](images/77/77bbf7683a8ecfbd6864ce82eede96dcab60480e9f5395ac38eae88218af8fde.svg)\n\nSince the LU factorization of $A$ is\n\n![](images/d7/d704b00f3e25fdd85fcf123baf0340f87b82125ae2ce0718891102c9da9968f2.svg)\nour equation $A\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{b}$ can be written as\n\n![](images/14/1481be73064ba910f27931cba3f6a252123090c055ed3d4eb3657fd9b9084c58.svg)\n\ni.e., \n\n$$\nLU\\mathbf{x}= \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nNow, suppose we set $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the above, we have\n\n$$\nL\\mathbf{y}= \\mathbf{b}.\n$$\n\nIn other words, we can reduce the problem to the following system of matrix equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}Ly=b \\\\ Ux=y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $L$ and $U$ are both triangular, each of these equations is easily solvable using back and forward substitution.\n\n- First, we solve $L\\mathbf{y} = \\mathbf{b}.$ Let's assume that ![](images/46/46e89549c82a3571050bf103d9f539cfd82c197c6c0ffef9161ed3c67cdbb4c0.svg) Then, we have\n![](images/af/af1f7463d07d4ddf3f25b8ae1cff3d658f8c0d2a9ab8295c5ea1e0e9c69d2fe6.svg)\nwhich gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}=-1 \\\\ -{y}_{1}+{y}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using *forward* substitution, we get the solution\n![](images/5b/5bb589d79fbb718f91b5df14ae4c7ac0a3767cb625c2079c0b16562b74484491.svg)\n\n- Finally, we solve $U\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{y}.$ We have\n![](images/4a/4a54f9bbd8830b3be687a168c8d9548466124669f8e7309ee18e1f05904fd084.svg)\nwhich gives the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}-3{x}_{2}=-1 \\\\ -{x}_{2}=-1.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation using *back* substitution, we get the solution\n![](images/ba/baf88432261a561613c0241b2d768e9cedf4f4eddaecaaf761b774c36c1ed541.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "2a20df11-b376-5315-8a2a-b65f70b54723" :tutorial/math-academy-id 3181 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "d790cb21-6bf5-548a-9699-06a28ab34cad" :step/math-academy-id 17370 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120800}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120664 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119799 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119610 :question/id #uuid "9552ad30-9d46-5884-9877-18ca8842c252" :question/math-academy-id "e-4775"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7634b3ac-f9e6-514e-87ba-e78590c9fa53" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120185} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186240947} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241146} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186241147} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302218} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186377114} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390757} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479110} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479113}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving a 2x2 Linear System Using LU Factorization"} :step/id #uuid "03156e06-274b-5254-b220-6c7dbdbfbea4" :step/math-academy-id 17371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120665 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119800 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119611 :question/id #uuid "24bc4954-462b-5066-83d4-8a4a87efc7b7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4777"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "9fa9fbb8-b2bc-5c93-bb07-bc8c7ef7c115" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049151} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050267} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051083}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120191} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186